{
  "slug": "next-greater-element-ii",
  "title": "Next Greater Element II",
  "topic": "stack",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "Monotonic stack over a doubled array",
  "statement": "For every position `i` of the circular list `nums`, look clockwise for the first value strictly greater than `nums[i]`: examine `nums[i+1]`, then `nums[i+2]`, and so on, wrapping back to the start of the list after the end, and stop at the first element that beats `nums[i]`. Elements at positions before `i` are only considered after the scan wraps around.\n\nReturn an array `res` of the same length as `nums` where `res[i]` is that first greater value, or `-1` when no element of the list is strictly greater than `nums[i]`. Duplicates are not greater than each other, and a position never compares against itself.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200` and `-10000 <= nums[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-10000]",
      "output": "[-1]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [-221, 6635, 9954, -3483]",
      "output": "[6635, 9954, -1, -221]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Baseline: for every index, walk clockwise up to n-1 steps using the modulo operator and stop at the first larger value.",
    "A single new value can answer several earlier indices at once, and those waiting indices always hold a non-increasing sequence of values.",
    "Keep a stack of unresolved indices and iterate i from 0 to 2n-1 using i mod n: pop and answer every index whose value is smaller than nums[i mod n], and push indices only while i < n."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force answers each index independently: for position i it walks up to n-1 steps clockwise with the modulo operator and stops at the first larger value, which costs O(n\u00b2) comparisons. The optimal version uses a monotonic stack of unresolved indices and walks the list twice, for i from 0 to 2n-1 with idx = i mod n. Whenever nums[idx] is larger than the value at the index on top of the stack, that index has found its answer and is popped with nums[idx] written into its slot. Indices are pushed only during the first pass, so after the second pass whatever is still on the stack has no greater element anywhere and keeps its initial -1. The values on the stack are always non-increasing from bottom to top, which is exactly the invariant that makes one pass enough.",
    "why_optimal": "Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so the double pass performs O(n) stack work, and the two passes themselves are O(n). That is the floor because the output has one entry per element and the answer for an index can be decided by any other element, including one that appears before it in the array. The duplicate second pass is what handles wraparound without a search: an element near the start whose answer lives behind it is resolved during the second traversal of the list.",
    "pitfalls": "The second pass must cover the full range from n to 2n-1; stopping early (for example at i < 2n-2) leaves large values near the start unanswered even though a greater element exists. Indices must not be pushed during the second pass, otherwise an index can be resolved against itself or the stack grows past n entries. The comparison has to be strict: with `<=` the stack pops equal values, and duplicates then receive an answer that the brute force would never give. Forgetting to initialise the answer array to -1 leaves undefined or 0 entries whenever no greater element exists."
  },
